    "done_when": "At completion, reckon/crew/promotion.py computes the run's review tier by calling reckon.review_tiers.review_tier with four inputs: the run's actual changed paths; their added-plus-deleted line count at the promoted head; the run's declared spec level; and the capability risk its plan or section declares, read through the existing plan parser. The light ceiling comes from the resolved review.tiers flight key, not a literal. It writes the tier to the ledger row as review_tier. _require_review_waiver reads that tier in place of the role check: a none-tier run promotes without a stored review, and its ledger row records the tier as the reason no review exists. A full-tier or light-tier run without a stored review at the promoted head is refused exactly as today, and a review run stays exempt. Dispatching the light review with its ten-minute budget, and escalating one that overruns, are out of scope. tests/test_promotion_reads_the_review_tier.py covers four runs in a synthesised repository: a tests-only run promotes unreviewed with review_tier none on its ledger row; a small runtime-source run at spec level exact is light, and is refused without a review; a large runtime-source run is full, and is refused without a review; and a small runtime-source run whose plan declares capability risk elevated is full. It passes at exit 0, as do tests/test_review_tiers.py and the existing promotion review-gate tests the change reaches. The manifest records base and head arms of those files, each log header printing its revision and reckon.__file__, with FAILED ids per arm. The declared negative control is applied, the tests-only case is observed to be refused, and that log's absolute path is in the manifest with an EXIT= line and the FAILED test id.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Make promotion compute each run's review tier, record it on the ledger row, and gate on it in place of the role check",
